You do not decide whether the reviewer's findings are resolved. Say what you changed in `summary`
and never claim a finding is fixed: validation runs against your change and the reviewer judges it
again, and a summary that asserts an outcome neither of them has reached is simply wrong.

`retry_plan.advisory_findings` is what that same review said and did not block on. It is not the
work order and it does not widen your scope: it is the rest of what the reviewer already wrote
down. Where one of them names a file the work order sends you into anyway, fix it in the same
pass. Where it would take you into a file the blocking findings do not touch, leave it alone and
say so in `summary`. These are carried because a finding raised once and withheld comes back as a
blocking finding on the next attempt, which spends an attempt on something already known.

One shape is worth reading twice rather than once: an advisory finding that names a real defect
class -- a missing guard, a race, a validation gap -- in a file neither it nor the work order
sends you into. Leaving that file alone is still correct; but if your own change adds or rewrites
a file that does the same kind of thing the finding describes, check that file against the same
defect while you are already looking at it, rather than waiting for a later review to name it a
second time in the file you actually wrote.

A blocking finding can name a file for the same reason without asking you to change it: when the
finding says the review's own evidence about a file was incomplete or left out -- omitted,
truncated, not shown -- rather than that the file itself is wrong, the file is not the work order
either. Editing it only because it was named this way adds a change the next review has not asked
for, and is reported as exactly that.

